**History:** Onset, duration, location, character, severity, associated symptoms (nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, visual changes, neck stiffness, fever, recent trauma, focal neurological deficits, recent illness).\n- **Family history:** Migraine or other headache disorders.\n- **Medication history:** Recent changes, over-the-counter or prescription drugs.\n- **Social history:** School stress, screen time, sleep patterns.\n- **Physical exam:** General appearance, vital signs, neurological exam, fundoscopic exam, sinus tenderness, neck stiffness.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- Sudden, severe (\"worst ever\") headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis)\n- Altered mental status, confusion, seizures\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision loss, ataxia)\n- Papilledema (signs of raised intracranial pressure)\n- Headache that awakens from sleep or is worse in the morning\n- Recent head trauma\n- Persistent vomiting\n- Immunosuppression or known malignancy\n\n---\n\n**Clinical Note:**  \nGiven the limited information, a thorough history and physical examination are essential to guide further workup. If any red flags are present, urgent evaluation is warranted. Otherwise, outpatient management and monitoring may be appropriate.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nAdvise prompt evaluation by a healthcare professional to further characterize the headache and rule out serious causes.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-09-08 05:33:27', NULL, 98),
(30, 53, '44', 'Headache', '**Patient Analysis: Headache in a 13-year-old Male**\n\n---\n\n**1. Potential Differential Diagnoses**\n\n- **Primary Headache Disorders**\n  - Migraine (with or without aura)\n  - Tension-type headache\n  - Cluster headache (less common in this age group)\n\n- **Secondary Headache Causes**\n  - Sinusitis\n  - Viral or bacterial infection (e.g., upper respiratory tract infection, meningitis)\n  - Medication overuse headache\n  - Refractive error (vision problems)\n  - Trauma (recent head injury)\n  - Intracranial pathology (tumor, hydrocephalus, hemorrhage)\n  - Hypertension\n  - Dental issues (e.g., temporomandibular joint dysfunction)\n  - Dehydration\n\n---\n\n**2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests**\n\n*Initial evaluation should be guided by history and physical examination. Consider the following:*\n\n- **Basic Workup**\n  - Detailed history and neurological examination\n  - Vital signs (including blood pressure)\n  - Fundoscopic examination (for papilledema)\n  - Sinus examination\n\n- **Further Investigations (if indicated by red flags or abnormal findings)**\n  - Neuroimaging (MRI or CT brain) if suspicion of intracranial pathology\n  - Lumbar puncture (if signs of meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage)\n  - CBC, CRP/ESR (if infection or inflammation suspected)\n  - Vision assessment (if visual symptoms present)\n\n---\n\n**3. Urgency Level**\n\n- **Medium** (based on current information)\n  - *If only headache is present without red flags or abnormal findings, outpatient evaluation is appropriate.*\n  - *If any red flags are present (see below), urgency increases to High.*\n\n---\n\n**4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes**\n\n- Age and gender: 13-year-old male\n- Symptom: Headache (no further details provided)\n- No past medical history of note (medical history entry appears non-specific)\n- No associated symptoms provided (e.g., fever, neck stiffness, vomiting, visual changes, trauma)\n- No mention of recent illness, trauma, or medication use\n\n*Clinical notes:*\n- A thorough history is essential (onset, duration, location, character, associated symptoms, triggers, family history of migraine, impact on daily activities).\n- Physical and neurological examination is critical to rule out secondary causes.\n\n---\n\n**5. Red Flags to Watch For**\n\n- Sudden, severe (\"thunderclap\") headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis/encephalitis)\n- Altered mental status, confusion, seizures\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision loss, ataxia)\n- Papilledema (suggests increased intracranial pressure)\n- Headache worsening with Valsalva, lying down, or in the morning\n- Recent head trauma\n- New onset headache in a patient with cancer or immunosuppression\n- Persistent vomiting\n- Headache that awakens from sleep\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nMr Patient, a 13-year-old male, presents with headache. The differential is broad and includes primary and secondary causes. The urgency is medium unless red flags are present. A detailed history and examination are essential to guide further workup. Immediate attention is warranted if any red flags develop.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nAdvise prompt evaluation by a healthcare professional for further assessment and management.', 'sinus', '', 'Certainly. Based on the information provided—including the clinical input of \"sinus\"—the most likely diagnosis is **acute sinusitis** (rhinosinusitis) as the cause of headache in this 13-year-old male. Below is a comprehensive, evidence-based treatment plan:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Primary Diagnosis\n\n**Acute Sinusitis (Acute Rhinosinusitis)**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Treatment Goals\n\n- Relieve headache and sinus symptoms\n- Eradicate infection (if bacterial)\n- Prevent complications (e.g., orbital or intracranial spread)\n- Restore normal sinus function\n- Minimize unnecessary antibiotic use\n\n---\n\n### 3. Medication Recommendations\n\n#### a. **First-line: Symptomatic Management**\n- **Analgesics/Antipyretics:**  \n  - *Acetaminophen* 10–15 mg/kg/dose every 4–6 hours as needed (max 75 mg/kg/day)\n  - *Ibuprofen* 5–10 mg/kg/dose every 6–8 hours as needed (max 40 mg/kg/day)\n\n- **Nasal Saline Irrigation:**  \n  - Isotonic saline nasal spray or irrigation 2–3 times daily\n\n- **Intranasal Corticosteroids** (if significant nasal congestion or allergic rhinitis):  \n  - *Fluticasone nasal spray* 1–2 sprays per nostril once daily\n\n#### b. **Antibiotics**  \n*Indicated only if:*\n- Symptoms persist >10 days without improvement\n- Severe symptoms (fever ≥39°C, purulent nasal discharge, facial pain) for ≥3 consecutive days\n- Worsening symptoms after initial improvement (\"double sickening\")\n\n**First-line antibiotic:**\n- *Amoxicillin-clavulanate* 45 mg/kg/day (amoxicillin component) divided every 12 hours for 10 days  \n  - If non-severe penicillin allergy: *Cefdinir* 14 mg/kg/day in 1–2 doses for 10 days  \n  - If severe allergy: *Levofloxacin* 10–20 mg/kg/day once daily for 10 days\n\n**Note:** Do not use macrolides due to resistance.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions\n\n- Encourage adequate hydration\n- Warm compresses over sinuses for pain relief\n- Rest\n- Avoid exposure to irritants (smoke, strong odors)\n- Humidified air (cool mist humidifier in bedroom)\n\n---\n\n### 5. Follow-up Schedule\n\n- **Routine:** Reassess in 48–72 hours if symptoms worsen or fail to improve\n- **Earlier:** Immediate evaluation if red flag symptoms develop (see below)\n- **After antibiotics:** Re-evaluate at end of course if not fully resolved\n\n---\n\n### 6. Monitoring Parameters\n\n- Symptom resolution (headache, nasal congestion, discharge, facial pain)\n- Temperature (fever resolution)\n- Adverse effects of medications (GI upset, rash, allergic reaction)\n- Signs of complications (see below)\n\n---\n\n### 7. Patient Education Points\n\n- Most sinus infections are viral and resolve without antibiotics\n- Use antibiotics only as prescribed; complete the full course if started\n- Proper technique for nasal saline irrigation\n- Recognize and report red flag symptoms promptly\n- Avoid overuse of decongestants (risk of rebound congestion)\n- Maintain good hand hygiene to prevent spread of infection\n\n---\n\n### 8. Potential Complications to Watch For\n\n- **Orbital complications:** Periorbital swelling, erythema, visual changes, ophthalmoplegia\n- **Intracranial complications:** Severe headache, altered mental status, persistent vomiting, neck stiffness, seizures\n- **Chronic sinusitis:** Symptoms >12 weeks\n- **Antibiotic adverse reactions:** Rash, diarrhea, anaphylaxis\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nMr Patient likely has acute sinusitis. Begin with symptomatic management; reserve antibiotics for persistent, severe, or worsening symptoms. Educate on red flags and ensure close follow-up. Consult ENT or refer to hospital if complications or red flags arise.\n\n**Always recommend consultation with a healthcare professional for confirmation of diagnosis and ongoing management.**', 'Medications:\n1. Acetaminophen 325 mg - 1 tablet by mouth every 4–6 hours as needed for pain or fever - Maximum 5 doses in 24 hours\n2. Isotonic saline nasal spray - 2 sprays per nostril - 2 to 3 times daily - For 10 days\n3. Fluticasone nasal spray 50 mcg - 1 spray per nostril - Once daily - For 10 days\n\nInstructions:\n- Take acetaminophen with or without food as needed for headache or fever. Do not exceed the maximum daily dose.\n- Use saline nasal spray to help clear nasal congestion.\n- Use fluticasone nasal spray at the same time each day. Shake well before use.\n- Drink plenty of fluids and get adequate rest.\n- Apply a warm compress over the sinuses for pain relief.\n\nPrecautions:\n- Do not use over-the-counter decongestant nasal sprays for more than 3 days to avoid rebound congestion.\n- Seek medical attention if symptoms worsen, do not improve after 10 days, or if you develop severe headache, swelling around the eyes, vision changes, persistent vomiting, neck stiffness, or rash.', 992, 992, '2025-09-08 05:37:00', '2025-09-08 05:38:35', 98);
INSERT INTO `treatment_plans` (`id`, `patient_id`, `doctor_id`, `symptoms`, `symptom_analysis`, `doctor_input`, `test_results`, `treatment_plan`, `prescription`, `created_by`, `updated_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `hospital_id`) VALUES
(31, 53, '44', 'headache', '**Patient Information:**  \nName: Mr Patient  \nAge: 13 years  \nGender: Male  \nMedical History: Not specified (text appears non-informative)\n\n**Presenting Symptom:**  \nHeadache\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\nIn a 13-year-old male presenting with headache, consider the following common and serious causes:\n\n- **Primary Headache Disorders:**\n  - Migraine (with or without aura)\n  - Tension-type headache\n  - Cluster headache (less common in this age group)\n\n- **Secondary Headache Causes:**\n  - Viral or bacterial infection (e.g., sinusitis, upper respiratory tract infection, meningitis)\n  - Medication overuse or withdrawal\n  - Refractive error (vision problems)\n  - Trauma (concussion, subdural/epidural hematoma)\n  - Intracranial mass or space-occupying lesion (rare, but important to consider)\n  - Hypertension\n  - Dental or temporomandibular joint (TMJ) issues\n  - Dehydration\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n**Initial Evaluation:**\n- **Thorough history and physical examination** (including neurological exam)\n- **Vital signs** (including blood pressure, temperature)\n\n**Further investigations based on clinical suspicion:**\n- **CBC, CRP/ESR** (if infection or inflammation suspected)\n- **Urinalysis** (if systemic symptoms)\n- **Ophthalmologic evaluation** (if visual symptoms)\n- **Neuroimaging (CT/MRI brain):**\n  - If red flags present (see below)\n  - Abnormal neurological findings\n  - Sudden onset (\"thunderclap\" headache)\n  - Headache worsened by Valsalva or position\n- **Lumbar puncture:** If meningitis or subarachnoid hemorrhage suspected and imaging is negative\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n**Medium**  \n- Most pediatric headaches are benign, but a careful assessment is required to rule out serious causes.\n- If any red flags are present (see below), urgency increases to **High**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- **History:** Onset, duration, frequency, location, quality, severity, associated symptoms (nausea, vomiting, photophobia, phonophobia, visual changes, fever, neck stiffness, recent trauma, altered mental status)\n- **Family history:** Migraines or neurological disorders\n- **Medication use:** Recent changes, overuse\n- **Social history:** School stress, sleep patterns, screen time, caffeine use\n- **Physical exam:** Full neurological exam, fundoscopic exam (for papilledema), assessment for meningeal signs, sinus tenderness\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- Sudden, severe (\"worst ever\") headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis/encephalitis)\n- Altered mental status, confusion, seizures\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision loss, ataxia)\n- Papilledema (suggests increased intracranial pressure)\n- Headache worsened by lying down, coughing, or Valsalva\n- Recent head trauma\n- Persistent vomiting\n- Headache awakening from sleep or present on awakening\n- Immunosuppression or known malignancy\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nA 13-year-old male with headache requires a thorough history and physical exam to distinguish between primary and secondary causes. Most headaches in this age group are benign, but red flags must be actively sought. If any are present, urgent evaluation and neuroimaging are warranted. Otherwise, outpatient workup and symptomatic management may be appropriate.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nFurther clinical details are needed. Please consult a healthcare professional for a comprehensive evaluation.', 'migrane', '', 'Certainly. Here is a comprehensive, structured treatment plan for a 13-year-old male diagnosed with **migraine**:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Primary Diagnosis\n\n**Migraine headache** (pediatric/adolescent onset)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Treatment Goals\n\n- Rapid relief of acute migraine attacks\n- Minimize headache frequency, severity, and duration\n- Prevent migraine-related disability (school absence, activity limitation)\n- Reduce reliance on acute medications and prevent medication overuse\n- Identify and manage triggers\n- Monitor for and prevent complications\n\n---\n\n## 3. Medication Recommendations\n\n### a. **Acute (Abortive) Treatment**\n\n**First-line:**\n- **Acetaminophen**: 15 mg/kg/dose PO, max 1000 mg/dose, every 4–6 hours as needed (max 75 mg/kg/day or 4000 mg/day)\n- **Ibuprofen**: 10 mg/kg/dose PO, max 600 mg/dose, every 6–8 hours as needed (max 40 mg/kg/day or 2400 mg/day)\n\n**If inadequate response and diagnosis confirmed:**\n- **Triptans** (approved for pediatric use; age restrictions apply):\n    - **Sumatriptan nasal spray**: 5–20 mg (start with 10 mg), may repeat once after 2 hours if needed (max 40 mg/24h)\n    - **Rizatriptan**: 5 mg PO (for ages 6–17 years, weight-based), may repeat once after 2 hours (max 10 mg/24h)\n\n**Note:** Avoid opioid and barbiturate-containing medications.\n\n### b. **Preventive (Prophylactic) Treatment**\n\nConsider if:\n- ≥4 disabling headaches/month\n- Significant impact on quality of life\n- Poor response to acute therapy\n\n**Options:**\n- **Propranolol**: 10–20 mg PO BID, titrate as needed (max 2–4 mg/kg/day)\n- **Amitriptyline**: 0.25–1 mg/kg PO at bedtime\n- **Topiramate**: 25 mg PO at bedtime, titrate up to 100 mg/day as tolerated\n\n**Selection based on comorbidities, side effect profile, and patient/family preference.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions\n\n- **Lifestyle modifications:**\n    - Regular sleep schedule\n    - Hydration (adequate fluid intake)\n    - Regular meals, avoid skipping meals\n    - Limit caffeine and screen time\n    - Regular physical activity\n- **Trigger identification and avoidance:** Keep a headache diary\n- **Stress management:** Relaxation techniques, cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) if indicated\n- **Biofeedback** (if available)\n- **Education** on early recognition and management of symptoms\n\n---\n\n## 5. Follow-up Schedule\n\n- **Initial follow-up:** 2–4 weeks after starting treatment or sooner if symptoms worsen\n- **Subsequent follow-ups:** Every 3 months if stable, or as clinically indicated\n- **Headache diary review** at each visit\n\n---\n\n## 6. Monitoring Parameters\n\n- **Headache frequency, severity, and duration** (headache diary)\n- **Medication use:** Frequency and response to abortive therapy\n- **Side effects** of medications (e.g., drowsiness, weight changes, mood changes)\n- **Impact on daily activities** (school attendance, participation in activities)\n- **Vital signs** (especially if on beta-blockers)\n- **Growth parameters** (height, weight, BMI)\n\n---\n\n## 7. Patient Education Points\n\n- **Nature of migraine:** Not dangerous, but can be disabling\n- **Importance of early treatment:** Take medication at onset of headache for best effect\n- **Avoid medication overuse:** Limit acute medications to ≤2–3 days/week to prevent rebound headaches\n- **Lifestyle factors:** Emphasize regular routines and trigger avoidance\n- **When to seek urgent care:** If headache is sudden/severe, associated with fever, neck stiffness, confusion, weakness, vision loss, or persistent vomiting\n\n---\n\n## 8. Potential Complications to Watch For\n\n- **Medication overuse headache** (rebound headache)\n- **Adverse effects** of medications (GI upset, sedation, mood changes, weight gain/loss, cognitive effects)\n- **Progression to chronic migraine**\n- **Psychosocial impact:** School absenteeism, anxiety, depression\n- **Development of new or atypical symptoms** (may indicate secondary cause)\n\n---\n\n**Note:**  \nThis plan should be individualized based on the patient’s full history, comorbidities, and response to therapy. All medication dosages should be confirmed and adjusted as appropriate for age and weight. **Consultation with a pediatric neurologist** is recommended for refractory or complex cases.\n\n**Always advise close follow-up and re-evaluation if symptoms change or do not improve.**', NULL, 992, 992, '2025-09-08 05:54:33', '2025-09-08 05:55:43', 98),
(32, 53, '44', 'RIght hand arm muscle pain ', '**Patient Information:**  \nName: Mr Patient  \nAge: 13 years  \nGender: Male  \nMedical History: Not specified (text appears as placeholder)\n\n**Presenting Complaint:**  \nRight hand/arm muscle pain\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\n- **Muscle strain or overuse injury** (most common in adolescents, especially if involved in sports or physical activity)\n- **Minor trauma or contusion** (history of recent injury, fall, or impact)\n- **Tendinitis or tenosynovitis**\n- **Nerve compression or entrapment** (e.g., carpal tunnel syndrome, though less common at this age)\n- **Infection** (cellulitis, abscess, or myositis—especially if associated with redness, swelling, or fever)\n- **Referred pain** (from cervical radiculopathy or shoulder pathology)\n- **Juvenile idiopathic arthritis** (if associated with joint swelling, morning stiffness)\n- **Compartment syndrome** (rare, but critical to rule out if severe pain, swelling, or neurovascular compromise)\n- **Bone pathology** (e.g., fracture, osteomyelitis, bone tumor—less likely but important to consider)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n- **Detailed physical examination** (inspection, palpation, range of motion, neurovascular assessment)\n- **Plain X-ray** of the right hand/arm (if trauma suspected or to rule out fracture)\n- **Ultrasound** (if soft tissue injury, abscess, or tenosynovitis suspected)\n- **CBC, ESR, CRP** (if infection or inflammatory process suspected)\n- **MRI** (if persistent, unexplained pain or suspicion of deep tissue involvement)\n- **Nerve conduction studies** (if neuropathic symptoms present)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n- **Low to Medium**  \n  - If pain is mild, no trauma, no systemic symptoms, and no neurovascular compromise.\n- **High**  \n  - If any red flags (see below) are present.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- **History:** Onset, duration, character, and location of pain; history of trauma or overuse; associated symptoms (swelling, redness, fever, numbness, weakness).\n- **Examination:** Look for swelling, erythema, warmth, deformity, tenderness, range of motion, and neurovascular status (capillary refill, pulses, sensation, motor function).\n- **Functional impact:** Ability to use the hand/arm, impact on daily activities.\n- **Systemic symptoms:** Fever, malaise, weight loss.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- **Severe, unrelenting pain** (especially out of proportion to exam)\n- **Rapidly increasing swelling or tightness** (suggestive of compartment syndrome)\n- **Neurovascular compromise** (numbness, tingling, weakness, pallor, absent pulses)\n- **Signs of infection** (fever, erythema, warmth, fluctuance)\n- **History of significant trauma**\n- **Systemic symptoms** (fever, weight loss, night sweats)\n- **Inability to move fingers/hand or loss of function**\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nRight hand/arm muscle pain in a 13-year-old male is most commonly due to minor trauma or overuse, but a thorough history and examination are essential to rule out more serious causes. Immediate attention is required if any red flags are present. Further workup should be guided by clinical findings.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nAdvise prompt clinical evaluation to determine the underlying cause and initiate appropriate management.', 'not sure', '', 'Certainly. Below is a comprehensive, structured treatment plan for a 13-year-old male presenting with right hand/arm muscle pain, based on the information provided. This plan assumes no red flags or systemic symptoms are present and that the most likely diagnosis is a **muscle strain or overuse injury**. If further history or examination suggests another etiology, the plan should be adjusted accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Primary Diagnosis\n\n**Muscle strain or overuse injury** of the right hand/arm  \n*(If new information arises—such as trauma, infection, or neurological symptoms—reassess and update diagnosis.)*\n\n---\n\n## 2. Treatment Goals\n\n- Relieve pain and restore function\n- Prevent further injury\n- Identify and address any underlying or serious pathology\n- Educate patient and family on injury prevention and warning signs\n\n---\n\n## 3. Medication Recommendations\n\n**First-line (if no contraindications):**\n- **Acetaminophen (Paracetamol):**  \n  - Dose: 10–15 mg/kg per dose every 4–6 hours as needed (maximum 75 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 3,000 mg/day)\n- **OR Ibuprofen:**  \n  - Dose: 5–10 mg/kg per dose every 6–8 hours as needed (maximum 40 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 2,400 mg/day)\n- **Duration:** Use for 3–5 days or until pain resolves. Taper as symptoms improve.\n\n**Note:**  \n- Avoid NSAIDs if there is suspicion of fracture, renal impairment, or GI issues.\n- Do not use both acetaminophen and ibuprofen simultaneously unless specifically indicated.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions\n\n- **Rest:** Limit activities that exacerbate pain for 48–72 hours.\n- **Ice:** Apply ice packs to the affected area for 15–20 minutes every 2–3 hours for the first 48 hours.\n- **Compression:** Use an elastic bandage if mild swelling is present (ensure no neurovascular compromise).\n- **Elevation:** Keep the arm elevated above heart level to reduce swelling.\n- **Gentle stretching and range-of-motion exercises:** Begin as pain allows to prevent stiffness.\n- **Physical therapy referral:** If pain persists beyond 1–2 weeks or if there is functional limitation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Follow-up Schedule\n\n- **Initial follow-up:** 3–5 days to reassess pain, function, and check for any new symptoms.\n- **Further follow-up:** 1–2 weeks if symptoms persist or worsen.\n- **Immediate review:** If any red flags develop (see below).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Monitoring Parameters\n\n- Pain severity and functional status\n- Swelling, redness, or warmth\n- Neurovascular status (sensation, movement, capillary refill)\n- Signs of infection (fever, local erythema, fluctuance)\n- Ability to perform daily activities\n\n---\n\n## 7. Patient Education Points\n\n- Importance of rest and gradual return to activity\n- Proper use of medications (dose, frequency, duration)\n- How to apply ice and compression safely\n- Warning signs that require urgent medical attention:\n  - Severe or worsening pain\n  - Numbness, tingling, or weakness\n  - Inability to move fingers/hand\n  - Swelling, redness, or warmth\n  - Fever or systemic symptoms\n- Avoiding reinjury: warm-up before activities, use proper technique, avoid overuse\n\n---\n\n## 8. Potential Complications to Watch For\n\n- **Compartment syndrome:** Severe pain, swelling, neurovascular compromise (medical emergency)\n- **Infection:** Increasing redness, warmth, swelling, fever\n- **Nerve injury:** Persistent numbness, tingling, or weakness\n- **Chronic pain or loss of function:** If not improving as expected\n- **Missed fracture or underlying pathology:** If pain persists or worsens despite treatment\n\n---\n\n**If any red flags or concerning features develop, or if the diagnosis is uncertain, escalate care and consider further diagnostic workup (imaging, labs, specialist referral).**\n\n---\n\n**Note:**  \nThis plan is for general guidance and should be tailored to the individual patient based on clinical findings and response to initial management. Always consult with a healthcare professional for definitive diagnosis and treatment.', 'Medications:\n1. Ibuprofen 200 mg (tablet or liquid, as appropriate) - Take 1 tablet (or 5–10 mg/kg per dose) by mouth every 6–8 hours as needed for pain - For up to 5 days\n\nInstructions:\n- Take with food to reduce stomach upset.\n- Do not exceed 40 mg/kg per day or 2,400 mg per day (whichever is lower).\n- Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration needed.\n- Rest the affected arm and avoid activities that cause pain for 48–72 hours.\n- Apply ice to the painful area for 15–20 minutes every 2–3 hours for the first 48 hours.\n- Elevate the arm above heart level to reduce swelling.\n\nPrecautions:\n- Do not use if you have a history of stomach ulcers, kidney problems, or allergy to ibuprofen.\n- Stop medication and seek medical attention if you develop severe pain, swelling, redness, numbness, tingling, weakness, fever, or if symptoms worsen or do not improve after 5 days.', 992, 992, '2025-09-08 06:10:28', '2025-09-08 06:12:38', 98),
(33, 53, '44', 'Server lower abdomen pain', '**Patient Analysis**\n\n**Patient Information:**  \n- Name: Mr Patient  \n- Age: 13 years  \n- Gender: Male  \n- Medical History: Not specified (text appears as \"dfgdfg\")\n\n**Current Symptom:**  \n- Severe lower abdominal pain\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\nFor a 13-year-old male with severe lower abdominal pain, consider the following:\n\n- **Acute Appendicitis**  \n  (Most common surgical emergency in this age group; pain often starts periumbilically and migrates to the right lower quadrant)\n- **Testicular Torsion**  \n  (Can present as lower abdominal pain, especially if referred pain; must be ruled out urgently)\n- **Gastroenteritis**  \n  (Usually associated with vomiting/diarrhea)\n- **Constipation/Fecal Impaction**\n- **Urinary Tract Infection (UTI) or Cystitis**\n- **Mesenteric Adenitis**  \n  (Common in children/adolescents, often mimics appendicitis)\n- **Intussusception**  \n  (More common in younger children, but possible)\n- **Inguinal Hernia (incarcerated/strangulated)**\n- **Meckel’s Diverticulitis**\n- **Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)**\n- **Henoch-Schönlein Purpura (HSP)**\n- **Trauma**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n- **Physical Examination:**  \n  - Abdominal exam (tenderness, rebound, guarding, masses, bowel sounds)\n  - Genital exam (testicular tenderness, swelling, cremasteric reflex)\n- **Laboratory Tests:**  \n  - CBC (leukocytosis)\n  - CRP/ESR (inflammation)\n  - Urinalysis (UTI, hematuria)\n  - Electrolytes, renal function\n- **Imaging:**  \n  - Abdominal ultrasound (appendicitis, intussusception, hernia, testicular torsion)\n  - Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler (if testicular torsion suspected)\n  - Abdominal X-ray (if obstruction/constipation suspected)\n- **Other:**  \n  - Stool studies (if diarrhea present)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n**High**  \n- Severe lower abdominal pain in a 13-year-old male is a potentially surgical emergency.  \n- Immediate evaluation is warranted to rule out appendicitis, testicular torsion, or other acute abdominal conditions.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- **Pain characteristics:** Onset, duration, location, migration, severity, associated symptoms (nausea, vomiting, fever, urinary symptoms, constipation/diarrhea)\n- **Associated symptoms:** Vomiting, fever, dysuria, hematuria, scrotal pain/swelling, rash, recent trauma\n- **Past medical/surgical history:** Not provided\n- **Vital signs:** Not provided (tachycardia, hypotension, fever are important)\n- **Physical findings:** Not provided (abdominal tenderness, peritonism, testicular findings)\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- **Signs of peritonitis:** Rebound tenderness, guarding, rigidity\n- **Hemodynamic instability:** Tachycardia, hypotension, pallor\n- **Testicular pain/swelling:** Absent cremasteric reflex, high-riding testis (testicular torsion)\n- **Persistent vomiting**\n- **Bloody stools or urine**\n- **Severe, worsening, or sudden-onset pain**\n- **Inability to pass urine or stool**\n- **Signs of sepsis:** Fever, altered mental status\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nSevere lower abdominal pain in a 13-year-old male is a high-urgency presentation. Immediate evaluation is required to rule out surgical emergencies such as appendicitis and testicular torsion. Prompt physical examination, laboratory workup, and imaging are recommended. Monitor for red flags and consult pediatric surgery/urology as indicated.\n\n**Note:** This analysis is for informational purposes only. Immediate clinical assessment by a healthcare professional is essential.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-09-08 12:29:08', NULL, 98),
(34, 53, '44', 'headache and acidity', '**Patient Analysis**\n\n**Patient Information:**  \n- Name: Mr Patient  \n- Age: 13 years  \n- Gender: Male  \n- Medical History: Not specified (entry \"dfgdfg\" is not interpretable)\n\n**Current Symptoms:**  \n- Headache  \n- Acidity (presumed to mean symptoms of dyspepsia, heartburn, or epigastric discomfort)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\n**A. Primary Headache Disorders**\n- Tension-type headache\n- Migraine (with or without aura)\n\n**B. Secondary Headache Causes**\n- Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) or gastritis with referred pain or associated headache\n- Dehydration or electrolyte imbalance (can cause both headache and dyspepsia)\n- Medication side effects (if any medications are being taken)\n- Infection (e.g., viral illness, sinusitis)\n- Stress or anxiety (can manifest as both headache and gastrointestinal symptoms)\n\n**C. Other Considerations**\n- Peptic ulcer disease (rare at this age, but possible)\n- Intracranial pathology (less likely, but must be considered if red flags present)\n- Metabolic causes (e.g., hypoglycemia)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n**Initial Evaluation:**\n- Detailed history and physical examination (including neurological and abdominal exams)\n- Review of medication and dietary history\n\n**Laboratory/Imaging (if indicated by history/exam):**\n- CBC, electrolytes (if infection or dehydration suspected)\n- Blood glucose (if hypoglycemia suspected)\n- H. pylori testing (if peptic symptoms are prominent and persistent)\n- Urinalysis (to rule out infection)\n- Neuroimaging (CT/MRI) only if red flags for secondary headache are present\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n**Low to Medium**  \n- If symptoms are mild, non-progressive, and without red flags, outpatient evaluation is appropriate.\n- **Urgency increases to High** if any red flags (see below) are present.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- The combination of headache and acidity is non-specific and may be related to benign causes such as stress, dietary factors, or viral illness.\n- In adolescents, functional dyspepsia and primary headache disorders are common.\n- Lack of detailed medical history limits assessment; further information on duration, severity, associated symptoms, and medication use is needed.\n- Consider psychosocial factors, especially in adolescents.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n**Headache Red Flags:**\n- Sudden onset (\"thunderclap\" headache)\n- Severe, worsening, or persistent headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis/encephalitis)\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision changes, altered consciousness)\n- Headache waking from sleep or worse in the morning (raised intracranial pressure)\n- Recent head trauma\n\n**Acidity/Dyspepsia Red Flags:**\n- Persistent vomiting\n- Hematemesis or melena (blood in vomit or stool)\n- Unintentional weight loss\n- Severe abdominal pain, guarding, or rigidity\n- Signs of dehydration\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nThis adolescent male presents with headache and acidity. Most likely causes are benign, but a thorough history and physical exam are essential to rule out serious pathology. Watch for red flags that would necessitate urgent evaluation. If symptoms persist, worsen, or are accompanied by concerning features, prompt referral to a healthcare professional is warranted.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nAdvise in-person evaluation by a healthcare provider for further assessment and management.', 'Migraine', '', 'Certainly. Here is a comprehensive, structured treatment plan for Mr Patient, a 13-year-old male presenting with headache and acidity, with a clinical impression of migraine:\n\n---\n\n## 1. Primary Diagnosis\n\n**Migraine (with possible associated dyspepsia/acidity)**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Treatment Goals\n\n- Achieve rapid and sustained relief of acute migraine attacks\n- Minimize headache frequency, severity, and duration\n- Address and alleviate associated gastrointestinal symptoms (acidity/dyspepsia)\n- Prevent medication overuse and related complications\n- Improve quality of life and school attendance/performance\n- Educate patient and family on migraine management and red flags\n\n---\n\n## 3. Medication Recommendations\n\n### A. Acute Migraine Management\n\n**First-line:**\n- **Acetaminophen (Paracetamol):**  \n  - Dose: 10–15 mg/kg per dose orally, every 4–6 hours as needed (maximum 75 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 4 g/day)\n- **OR Ibuprofen:**  \n  - Dose: 10 mg/kg per dose orally, every 6–8 hours as needed (maximum 40 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 2.4 g/day)\n\n**If moderate-severe attacks or poor response to above:**\n- **Triptans (for age ≥12 years):**  \n  - **Sumatriptan nasal spray:** 10–20 mg per dose, may repeat once after 2 hours if needed (maximum 40 mg/24 hours)\n  - **Note:** Triptans are generally safe in adolescents but should be used under specialist guidance.\n\n### B. Management of Acidity/Dyspepsia\n\n- **Antacid (short-term, as needed):**  \n  - E.g., calcium carbonate or magnesium/aluminum hydroxide preparations, per package instructions for age/weight\n- **Consider H2 blocker or PPI** if symptoms are persistent and not relieved by antacids (e.g., **Ranitidine 2–4 mg/kg/day divided BID** or **Omeprazole 0.7–3.5 mg/kg/day once daily**), but only after physician evaluation.\n\n### C. Preventive Therapy\n\n- **Not indicated** unless headaches are frequent (≥4/month), disabling, or refractory to acute therapy. If needed, options include propranolol, amitriptyline, or topiramate—initiate only under specialist care.\n\n**Duration:**  \n- Acute medications: Use at onset of headache, limit to ≤2–3 days/week to avoid medication overuse headache.\n- Antacids: Short-term (days to 1–2 weeks); if longer needed, re-evaluate.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions\n\n- **Lifestyle Modifications:**\n  - Maintain regular sleep schedule\n  - Encourage hydration (adequate fluid intake)\n  - Regular, balanced meals; avoid skipping meals\n  - Identify and avoid known migraine triggers (e.g., certain foods, stress, dehydration)\n  - Limit caffeine and processed foods\n- **Headache Diary:**  \n  - Record frequency, duration, severity, triggers, and response to treatment\n- **Stress Management:**  \n  - Relaxation techniques, mindfulness, cognitive-behavioral therapy if indicated\n- **Screen Time:**  \n  - Limit excessive screen time, ensure regular breaks\n\n---\n\n## 5. Follow-up Schedule\n\n- **Initial follow-up:** 2–4 weeks after starting treatment or sooner if symptoms worsen\n- **Subsequent follow-up:** Every 3–6 months if stable, or as needed based on symptom control\n- **Immediate review:** If red flag symptoms develop (see below)\n\n---\n\n## 6. Monitoring Parameters\n\n- Headache frequency, severity, and duration (via diary)\n- Response to acute and supportive treatments\n- Frequency of medication use (to avoid overuse)\n- Side effects of medications (e.g., GI upset, drowsiness)\n- Growth parameters (if on long-term medications)\n- School attendance and daily functioning\n\n---\n\n## 7. Patient Education Points\n\n- Explain the nature of migraine and its benign prognosis in most cases\n- Importance of early treatment at headache onset\n- Avoid overuse of pain medications (risk of rebound/medication overuse headache)\n- Recognize and avoid personal triggers\n- When to seek urgent medical attention (see red flags below)\n- Importance of regular follow-up\n\n---\n\n## 8. Potential Complications to Watch For\n\n- **Medication overuse headache** (from frequent analgesic use)\n- **Gastrointestinal side effects** (from NSAIDs or antacids)\n- **Progression to chronic migraine** (if frequent, untreated)\n- **Development of red flag symptoms** (see below)\n- **Psychosocial impact** (missed school, anxiety, depression)\n\n---\n\n### **Red Flags (Require Urgent Evaluation):**\n\n- Sudden, severe (\"worst ever\") headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision loss, confusion)\n- Headache waking from sleep or worse in the morning\n- Persistent vomiting, dehydration, GI bleeding (hematemesis, melena)\n- Unintentional weight loss\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nMr Patient’s presentation is most consistent with migraine, possibly with associated functional dyspepsia. Management should focus on acute symptom relief, lifestyle modification, and education, with close monitoring for complications or red flag symptoms. If symptoms persist, worsen, or new concerning features develop, prompt re-evaluation is warranted.\n\n**All recommendations should be reviewed and tailored by the patient’s primary care provider or pediatric neurologist.**', 'Medications:\n1. Acetaminophen (Paracetamol) 500 mg tablets – 1 tablet by mouth every 4–6 hours as needed for headache – Do not exceed 4 times in 24 hours; maximum 2–3 days per week\n2. Calcium Carbonate chewable tablets 500 mg – 1 tablet by mouth as needed for acidity – Maximum 2 times per day; use for up to 1 week\n\nInstructions:\n- Take acetaminophen at the first sign of headache. Swallow with water. Do not take more than 4 doses in 24 hours.\n- Take calcium carbonate after meals or when experiencing acidity. Chew tablet thoroughly before swallowing.\n- Maintain a headache diary to track frequency, severity, and possible triggers.\n- Drink plenty of water and eat regular, balanced meals. Avoid skipping meals.\n\nPrecautions:\n- Do not use pain medications more than 2–3 days per week to avoid medication overuse headache.\n- If headaches become more frequent, severe, or are associated with red flag symptoms (such as sudden severe headache, vision changes, weakness, persistent vomiting, or fever), seek medical attention immediately.\n- If acidity persists beyond 1 week or is associated with vomiting blood or black stools, stop medication and consult a doctor.\n- Avoid known migraine triggers such as certain foods, dehydration, and excessive screen time. Ensure regular sleep and stress management.', 992, 992, '2025-09-08 23:15:06', '2025-09-08 23:16:46', 98),
(35, 53, '44', 'headche , acidity', '**Patient Analysis**\n\n**Patient Information:**  \n- Name: Mr Patient  \n- Age: 13 years  \n- Gender: Male  \n- Medical History: Not specified (text appears non-informative)\n\n**Current Symptoms:**  \n- Headache  \n- Acidity (likely refers to symptoms of dyspepsia, heartburn, or epigastric discomfort)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\n**A. Primary Headache Disorders**\n- Tension-type headache\n- Migraine (with or without aura)\n\n**B. Secondary Headache Causes**\n- Sinusitis (especially if associated with nasal symptoms)\n- Medication overuse headache\n- Dehydration or sleep deprivation\n\n**C. Gastrointestinal Causes**\n- Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)\n- Gastritis or peptic ulcer disease\n- Functional dyspepsia\n\n**D. Systemic Causes**\n- Viral illness (e.g., viral gastroenteritis, influenza)\n- Stress or anxiety (psychosomatic symptoms)\n\n**E. Less Common but Serious Causes**\n- Intracranial pathology (e.g., mass, infection, hemorrhage)\n- Hypertension (rare in this age group, but possible)\n- Metabolic disturbances (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis, though less likely with these symptoms alone)\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n**Initial Evaluation:**\n- Detailed history and physical examination (including neurological and abdominal exams)\n- Assessment of headache characteristics (onset, duration, location, associated symptoms)\n- Assessment of GI symptoms (relation to food, timing, associated nausea/vomiting, weight loss, GI bleeding)\n\n**Laboratory/Imaging (if indicated by history/exam):**\n- CBC, electrolytes (if systemic illness suspected)\n- H. pylori testing (if peptic ulcer suspected)\n- Upper GI endoscopy (if alarm symptoms present)\n- Brain imaging (CT/MRI) if red flags for secondary headache are present\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n**Low to Medium**  \n- If symptoms are mild, intermittent, and not associated with red flags, outpatient evaluation is appropriate.\n- **Urgency increases to High** if any red flags (see below) are present.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- The combination of headache and acidity is non-specific but may be related to lifestyle, stress, or a viral illness.\n- In adolescents, primary headache disorders and functional GI symptoms are common.\n- Lack of detailed medical history and absence of associated symptoms (fever, vomiting, neurological deficits, GI bleeding) limits specificity.\n- Consider psychosocial factors (school stress, anxiety) in this age group.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n**Headache Red Flags:**\n- Sudden, severe (\"thunderclap\") headache\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis/encephalitis)\n- Focal neurological deficits (weakness, vision changes, altered consciousness)\n- Headache worse with lying down or waking from sleep (raised intracranial pressure)\n- Recent head trauma\n\n**GI Red Flags:**\n- Persistent vomiting\n- Hematemesis or melena (GI bleeding)\n- Unintentional weight loss\n- Severe abdominal pain, guarding, or rigidity\n- Dysphagia or odynophagia\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nThis 13-year-old male presents with headache and acidity. Most likely causes are benign (primary headache, functional dyspepsia, stress). However, a thorough history and physical exam are essential to rule out serious causes. Watch for red flags that would necessitate urgent evaluation. Recommend follow-up with a healthcare professional for further assessment and management.', 'MIGRANE', '', 'Certainly. Here is a comprehensive, structured treatment plan for a 13-year-old male diagnosed with **migraine** and reporting symptoms of \"acidity\" (likely dyspepsia/GERD):\n\n---\n\n## 1. Primary Diagnosis\n\n**Migraine** (with or without aura)  \n*Acidity* (likely functional dyspepsia or mild GERD; monitor for evolution)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Treatment Goals\n\n- **Acute relief** of migraine attacks\n- **Prevention** of future migraine episodes (if frequent/severe)\n- **Control of dyspeptic symptoms**\n- **Minimize medication side effects**\n- **Improve quality of life and school attendance**\n- **Educate patient and family** on symptom management and red flags\n\n---\n\n## 3. Medication Recommendations\n\n### A. Migraine\n\n#### a. Acute (Abortive) Therapy\n- **Acetaminophen**: 10–15 mg/kg/dose PO every 4–6 hours as needed (max 75 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 3,000 mg/day)\n- **OR Ibuprofen**: 10 mg/kg/dose PO every 6–8 hours as needed (max 40 mg/kg/day, not to exceed 2,400 mg/day)\n- **Consider triptans** (e.g., sumatriptan nasal spray 5–20 mg, may repeat once in 2 hours; max 40 mg/day) if above ineffective and age-appropriate (sumatriptan nasal spray is FDA-approved for children ≥12 years)\n\n#### b. Preventive Therapy\n- **Indicated if**: ≥4 disabling headaches/month, poor response to abortive therapy, or significant impact on daily life\n- **Options** (start low, titrate up):\n    - **Propranolol**: 10–20 mg PO BID (monitor for hypotension, bradycardia, asthma)\n    - **Amitriptyline**: 0.25–1 mg/kg PO at bedtime (monitor for sedation, anticholinergic effects)\n    - **Topiramate**: 0.5–1 mg/kg/day PO in divided doses (monitor for cognitive effects, weight loss)\n- **Duration**: Reassess after 3–6 months\n\n### B. Dyspepsia/Acidity\n\n- **Lifestyle/dietary modification** (see below)\n- **If symptoms persist**:\n    - **Antacid** (e.g., calcium carbonate 500–1,000 mg PO as needed, max 3,000 mg/day)\n    - **OR H2 blocker** (e.g., famotidine 0.5 mg/kg PO BID, max 40 mg/day)\n    - **OR PPI** (e.g., omeprazole 0.7–3.5 mg/kg/day PO, max 20 mg/day) for 2–4 weeks if symptoms are frequent/severe\n\n**Note:** Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest duration. Avoid chronic use without further evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions\n\n### Migraine\n- **Identify and avoid triggers**: stress, sleep deprivation, dehydration, certain foods (chocolate, cheese, processed meats, caffeine)\n- **Regular sleep schedule**\n- **Hydration**: Encourage adequate fluid intake\n- **Balanced diet**\n- **Stress management**: Relaxation techniques, cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) if indicated\n- **Headache diary**: Track frequency, duration, triggers, and response to treatment\n\n### Dyspepsia/Acidity\n- **Avoid trigger foods**: spicy, fatty, acidic foods, carbonated beverages, caffeine\n- **Eat smaller, more frequent meals**\n- **Avoid eating late at night**\n- **Elevate head of bed** if symptoms at night\n- **Weight management** if overweight\n\n---\n\n## 5. Follow-up Schedule\n\n- **Initial follow-up**: 2–4 weeks after starting therapy or sooner if symptoms worsen\n- **Subsequent visits**: Every 3 months if stable, or as needed for medication adjustment\n- **Immediate review** if red flag symptoms develop\n\n---\n\n## 6. Monitoring Parameters\n\n- **Headache frequency, severity, and duration** (headache diary)\n- **Response to acute and preventive medications**\n- **Side effects** of medications (GI upset, sedation, mood changes, weight changes, vital signs)\n- **Dyspepsia symptom control**\n- **Growth parameters** (height, weight, BMI)\n- **School attendance and daily functioning**\n\n---\n\n## 7. Patient Education Points\n\n- **Nature of migraine**: Not dangerous, but can be disabling; often improves with age\n- **Importance of trigger avoidance and lifestyle modification**\n- **Proper use of medications**: Take at onset of headache for best effect; do not overuse (risk of medication overuse headache)\n- **When to seek urgent care**: Severe, sudden headache; persistent vomiting; neurological symptoms; GI bleeding; weight loss\n- **Keep a headache and symptom diary**\n- **Adherence**: Take medications as prescribed; do not stop abruptly without consulting provider\n\n---\n\n## 8. Potential Complications to Watch For\n\n- **Medication overuse headache** (from frequent use of analgesics)\n- **Gastritis or GI bleeding** (from NSAIDs)\n- **Side effects** of preventive medications (mood changes, sedation, weight changes, hypotension)\n- **Progression or change in headache pattern** (may indicate secondary cause)\n- **Development of red flag symptoms** (see above)\n- **Impact on school performance or psychosocial health**\n\n---\n\n**Note:**  \nThis plan should be individualized based on further history, examination, and response to therapy. All medication dosages should be double-checked and adjusted for the patient’s weight and clinical context. **Consultation with a pediatrician or pediatric neurologist is recommended for ongoing management.**\n\n---\n\n**References:**  \n- AAN/AHS Guidelines for Pediatric Migraine  \n- Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 21st Edition  \n- UpToDate: \"Acute treatment of migraine in children\" and \"Preventive treatment of migraine in children\"', 'Medications:\n1. Ibuprofen 200 mg (if weight ≥20 kg; adjust if less) - Take 1 tablet by mouth at onset of headache, may repeat every 6–8 hours as needed - Maximum 3 doses in 24 hours, do not exceed 600 mg/day - Use as needed for migraine attacks\n2. Famotidine 20 mg - Take 1 tablet by mouth twice daily - For 2 weeks\n\nInstructions:\n- Take ibuprofen with food or milk to reduce stomach upset.\n- Take famotidine 30 minutes before breakfast and dinner.\n- Keep a headache diary to track frequency, duration, and possible triggers.\n- Drink plenty of water and maintain a regular sleep schedule.\n- Avoid known migraine and acidity triggers (such as chocolate, cheese, spicy/fatty foods, caffeine, and carbonated drinks).\n- Eat smaller, more frequent meals and avoid eating late at night.\n\nPrecautions:\n- Do not use ibuprofen for more than 3 days per week to avoid medication overuse headache.\n- Stop ibuprofen and seek medical attention if you experience stomach pain, vomiting blood, black stools, or any allergic reaction.\n- If headaches become more frequent, severe, or are associated with vision changes, weakness, or persistent vomiting, seek medical attention promptly.\n- Do not stop medications abruptly without consulting your healthcare provider.', 992, 992, '2025-09-11 00:35:34', '2025-09-11 00:38:05', 98),
(36, 54, '44', 'dsfsdf', 'Patient Information  \nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon  \nAge: 33  \nGender: Male  \nMedical History: dfgdfg (insufficient/unclear)  \n\nCurrent Symptoms: dsfsdf (insufficient/unclear)  \n\n---\n\n**1. Potential Differential Diagnoses**  \nUnable to provide differential diagnoses due to lack of specific symptom information. Please provide clear and detailed symptom descriptions (e.g., fever, cough, chest pain, abdominal pain, etc.).\n\n**2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests**  \nCannot recommend specific diagnostic tests without knowing the presenting symptoms. General recommendations:  \n- Obtain a thorough history and physical examination  \n- Basic laboratory tests (CBC, metabolic panel) as indicated by symptoms  \n- Additional tests based on specific complaints\n\n**3. Urgency Level**  \nUnable to determine urgency without symptom details. If the patient is experiencing any of the following, consider as HIGH urgency:  \n- Chest pain  \n- Shortness of breath  \n- Severe abdominal pain  \n- Sudden neurological deficits  \n- Severe allergic reaction  \n- Uncontrolled bleeding\n\n**4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes**  \n- Medical history and current symptoms are not clearly provided.  \n- Accurate and detailed symptom description is essential for clinical assessment.  \n- Please clarify or correct the medical history and current symptoms.\n\n**5. Red Flags to Watch For**  \n- Sudden onset of severe symptoms (pain, shortness of breath, confusion, weakness)  \n- Signs of shock (pallor, sweating, rapid pulse, low blood pressure)  \n- Persistent vomiting or diarrhea  \n- High fever with altered mental status  \n- Any rapidly worsening condition\n\n---\n\n**Clinical Note:**  \nThe information provided is insufficient for a meaningful medical analysis. Please update the patient’s medical history and current symptoms with clear, specific details to allow for an accurate assessment and recommendations.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nConsult a healthcare professional promptly if the patient is experiencing any severe or concerning symptoms.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-09-15 00:30:45', NULL, 98),
(37, 54, '44', 'Headache', '**Patient Name:** Rizvi Mahmud Plabon  \n**Age:** 33  \n**Gender:** Male  \n**Medical History:** Not specified (entry \"dfgdfg\" is not interpretable)  \n**Presenting Symptom:** Headache\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\nGiven the limited information (isolated headache, no further history), the differential is broad and includes:\n\n- **Primary Headache Disorders:**\n  - Tension-type headache\n  - Migraine (with or without aura)\n  - Cluster headache\n\n- **Secondary Headache Causes:**\n  - Sinusitis\n  - Medication overuse headache\n  - Cervicogenic headache\n  - Hypertension (especially if severe)\n  - Infection (e.g., viral illness, meningitis)\n  - Intracranial pathology (e.g., tumor, hemorrhage, venous sinus thrombosis)\n  - Temporal arteritis (rare at this age, but consider if symptoms suggest)\n  - Trauma-related headache\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n**Initial Evaluation:**\n- **Detailed history and physical/neurological examination** (to identify red flags and guide further workup)\n\n**If no red flags and primary headache suspected:**\n- No immediate tests required; consider headache diary\n\n**If red flags or secondary headache suspected:**\n- **Neuroimaging:** Non-contrast CT head (acute), MRI brain (subacute/chronic)\n- **Laboratory tests:** CBC, ESR/CRP (if infection or temporal arteritis suspected)\n- **Lumbar puncture:** If meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, or idiopathic intracranial hypertension suspected\n- **Sinus imaging:** If sinusitis suspected\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n- **Low** if headache is chronic, stable, and without red flags.\n- **Medium to High** if any red flags are present (see below), or if new, severe, or sudden-onset headache.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- **History is critical:** Onset, duration, character, location, associated symptoms (nausea, photophobia, visual changes, fever, neck stiffness, focal neurological deficits, altered mental status, trauma, etc.)\n- **Examination:** Vital signs, fundoscopic exam (papilledema), full neurological exam, signs of meningeal irritation, sinus tenderness.\n- **Medication and substance use:** Recent changes, overuse, withdrawal.\n- **Family history:** Migraine, vascular disorders.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- Sudden onset (\"thunderclap\" headache)\n- Worst headache of life\n- New headache in patient >50 years old (not applicable here, but important)\n- Headache with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia (meningitis/encephalitis)\n- Headache with focal neurological deficits (stroke, mass lesion)\n- Headache with altered mental status\n- Headache with papilledema (raised intracranial pressure)\n- Headache after trauma\n- Headache with visual changes or jaw claudication\n- Immunosuppression or cancer history\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nWith only \"headache\" as a symptom and no further history, the differential is broad. A detailed history and examination are essential to narrow the diagnosis and determine the need for urgent investigation. If any red flags are present, urgent evaluation and neuroimaging are warranted. If the headache is typical of a primary headache disorder and no red flags are present, outpatient management and follow-up may be appropriate.\n\n**Recommendation:**  \nObtain a thorough history and perform a focused physical and neurological examination. Escalate care if any red flags are identified. Always advise the patient to seek immediate medical attention if symptoms worsen or new concerning features develop.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-09-15 00:45:54', NULL, 98);
INSERT INTO `treatment_plans` (`id`, `patient_id`, `doctor_id`, `symptoms`, `symptom_analysis`, `doctor_input`, `test_results`, `treatment_plan`, `prescription`, `created_by`, `updated_by`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `hospital_id`) VALUES
(38, 54, '44', '\npainfull red eye, joint pain, painfull rash at leg', '**Patient Name:** Rizvi Mahmud Plabon  \n**Age:** 33  \n**Gender:** Male  \n**Medical History:** Not specified (text appears as \"dfgdfg\")  \n**Current Symptoms:** Painful red eye, joint pain, painful rash on leg\n\n---\n\n### 1. Potential Differential Diagnoses\n\n**a. Reactive Arthritis (Reiter’s Syndrome)**\n- Classic triad: conjunctivitis/uveitis (painful red eye), arthritis (joint pain), and mucocutaneous lesions (rash).\n- Often follows genitourinary or gastrointestinal infection.\n\n**b. Disseminated Gonococcal Infection**\n- Can present with migratory polyarthritis, tenosynovitis, skin lesions (pustular or vesicular rash), and sometimes conjunctivitis.\n\n**c. Psoriatic Arthritis**\n- May present with joint pain and psoriatic skin lesions, which can be painful. Ocular involvement (uveitis) is possible.\n\n**d. Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)**\n- Can present with joint pain, skin rash (often malar, but can be elsewhere), and ocular involvement.\n\n**e. Vasculitis (e.g., Behçet’s Disease)**\n- Can present with painful oral/genital ulcers, uveitis, arthritis, and skin lesions.\n\n**f. Septic Arthritis with Associated Cellulitis**\n- If the rash is more consistent with cellulitis, and there is joint pain and red eye (possibly from bacteremia).\n\n**g. Erythema Nodosum with Associated Systemic Disease**\n- Painful nodular rash (often on shins), joint pain, and sometimes ocular involvement (seen in sarcoidosis, IBD, infections).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests\n\n- **Basic Blood Work:** CBC, ESR, CRP\n- **Autoimmune Panel:** ANA, RF, HLA-B27\n- **Infectious Workup:** Urethral/cervical swab, urine PCR for Chlamydia/Gonorrhea, blood cultures if febrile\n- **Joint Aspiration:** If joint is swollen, for cell count, Gram stain, culture, and crystals\n- **Skin Lesion Biopsy:** If diagnosis unclear\n- **Ophthalmologic Evaluation:** Slit lamp exam to assess for uveitis, conjunctivitis, or scleritis\n- **Imaging:** X-ray or ultrasound of affected joint(s) if indicated\n- **Other:** Chest X-ray (if sarcoidosis or TB suspected)\n\n---\n\n### 3. Urgency Level\n\n**Medium to High**\n\n- **High** if there is vision loss, severe eye pain, photophobia, or signs of septic arthritis (hot, swollen joint, fever).\n- **Medium** if symptoms are stable but persistent; prompt evaluation is still warranted due to risk of vision loss and joint damage.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes\n\n- The combination of painful red eye, joint pain, and painful rash is suggestive of a systemic inflammatory or infectious process.\n- The pattern is most consistent with a spondyloarthropathy (e.g., reactive arthritis) or a disseminated infection.\n- Ocular involvement can be vision-threatening and requires urgent ophthalmology input.\n- The nature of the rash (morphology, distribution) is important for narrowing the differential.\n- Absence of detailed medical history is a limitation; further history (recent infections, sexual history, travel, systemic symptoms) is needed.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Red Flags to Watch For\n\n- **Ocular:** Sudden vision loss, severe photophobia, purulent discharge, inability to open eye\n- **Joint:** Hot, swollen, erythematous joint (suggests septic arthritis)\n- **Systemic:** High fever, hypotension, tachycardia (sepsis)\n- **Skin:** Rapidly spreading rash, necrosis, bullae\n- **Other:** Meningismus, altered mental status\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nThis patient’s constellation of symptoms is concerning for a systemic inflammatory or infectious process, with possible vision- and joint-threatening complications. Urgent evaluation, including ophthalmology and rheumatology/infectious disease input, is recommended. Immediate attention is required if any red flags are present.\n\n**Note:** This analysis is for informational purposes only. A thorough clinical assessment by a healthcare professional is essential for diagnosis and management.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-10-08 00:46:03', NULL, 98),
(39, 54, '44', 'fsdfs', 'Patient Information  \nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon  \nAge: 33  \nGender: Male  \nMedical History: dfgdfg (not interpretable)\n\nCurrent Symptoms: fsdfs (not interpretable)\n\n---\n\n**Medical Analysis**\n\nUnfortunately, the provided medical history and current symptoms are not interpretable (\"dfgdfg\", \"fsdfs\"). As such, a specific clinical analysis cannot be performed. However, I will outline a general approach for cases where patient information is incomplete or unclear.\n\n---\n\n**1. Potential Differential Diagnoses**  \n*Unable to generate a differential diagnosis without specific symptoms or relevant medical history.*\n\n**2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests**  \n- Obtain a thorough history and clarify presenting symptoms.\n- Perform a comprehensive physical examination.\n- Basic laboratory tests (CBC, metabolic panel, urinalysis) as initial screening if the patient is unwell.\n- Additional tests as guided by clarified symptoms.\n\n**3. Urgency Level**  \n- **Unknown**: Unable to determine urgency without symptom details.\n- If the patient is acutely unwell (e.g., chest pain, shortness of breath, altered mental status), treat as **High Urgency** and seek immediate medical attention.\n\n**4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes**  \n- The provided information is insufficient for clinical assessment.\n- Clarification of symptoms and medical history is essential.\n- Consider language or data entry errors; re-interview the patient if possible.\n\n**5. Red Flags to Watch For**  \n- Chest pain, severe headache, shortness of breath, syncope, confusion, severe abdominal pain, uncontrolled bleeding, or any rapidly deteriorating condition.\n- If any of these are present, escalate care immediately.\n\n---\n\n**Summary**  \nThe current information is not interpretable. Please obtain a clear, detailed history and symptom description to proceed with a meaningful clinical analysis. If the patient appears acutely ill or unstable, treat as a medical emergency and seek immediate evaluation.\n\n**Note:** Always consult with a healthcare professional for accurate diagnosis and management.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-10-09 20:06:03', NULL, 98),
(40, 54, '44', 'fever', 'Patient Information:\nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon\nAge: 33\nGender: Male\nMedical History: Not clearly provided\n\nCurrent Symptoms:\nFever\n\n1. Potential Differential Diagnoses:\n   - Viral infection (e.g., influenza, common cold)\n   - Bacterial infection (e.g., strep throat, urinary tract infection)\n   - COVID-19\n   - Malaria\n   - Dengue fever\n   - Other systemic infections\n   - Autoimmune diseases\n   - Malignancies\n\n2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests:\n   - Complete Blood Count (CBC)\n   - Blood culture\n   - Urinalysis\n   - Chest X-ray (if respiratory symptoms are present)\n   - COVID-19 PCR test\n   - Malaria and Dengue tests (if patient lives in or has traveled to endemic areas)\n   - Other specific tests based on physical examination and patient\'s medical history\n\n3. Urgency Level: \n   - Medium: Fever is a common symptom of many conditions, some of which can be serious. It\'s important to identify the cause and start appropriate treatment.\n\n4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes:\n   - The patient\'s medical history is unclear, which limits the ability to narrow down potential causes of the fever. More information is needed about the duration of the fever, associated symptoms, recent travel, vaccination status, and potential exposure to infectious diseases.\n   - Monitor the patient\'s temperature and overall condition. \n\n5. Red Flags to Watch For:\n   - High fever (>103°F or 39.4°C)\n   - Fever lasting more than a week\n   - Severe headache\n   - Neck stiffness\n   - Shortness of breath\n   - Chest pain\n   - Abdominal pain\n   - Confusion or altered mental status\n   - Rash\n   - Any other severe or worsening symptoms\n\nPlease note that this analysis is based on the limited information provided and should not replace a comprehensive evaluation by a healthcare professional. The patient should seek immediate medical attention if symptoms worsen or if any red flags are present.', 'viral infection', '', 'Based on the provided information, here is a comprehensive treatment plan:\n\n1. Primary Diagnosis:\n   - Viral Infection\n\n2. Treatment Goals:\n   - Alleviate symptoms\n   - Prevent complications\n   - Prevent transmission to others\n\n3. Medication Recommendations:\n   - Acetaminophen 500 mg every 4-6 hours as needed for fever. Do not exceed 3000 mg in 24 hours.\n   - Over-the-counter cold remedies may help with other symptoms if present (e.g., cough, congestion). Follow package instructions for dosing.\n   - Note: Antibiotics are not effective against viral infections and are not recommended unless a secondary bacterial infection is diagnosed.\n\n4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions:\n   - Rest and hydration\n   - Isolation to prevent transmission to others until fever has been absent for at least 24 hours without the use of fever-reducing medications\n\n5. Follow-Up Schedule:\n   - If symptoms worsen or persist for more than a week, seek immediate medical attention.\n   - If symptoms improve, a follow-up visit in 2 weeks is recommended to ensure complete recovery.\n\n6. Monitoring Parameters:\n   - Monitor temperature daily\n   - Monitor for symptom progression or the development of new symptoms\n\n7. Patient Education Points:\n   - Understand the nature of viral infections and the importance of rest and hydration\n   - Understand the importance of preventing transmission to others\n   - Understand when to seek medical attention (e.g., worsening symptoms, high fever, symptoms persisting for more than a week)\n\n8. Potential Complications to Watch For:\n   - Secondary bacterial infection\n   - Dehydration due to fever and decreased oral intake\n   - In rare cases, viral infections can lead to more serious conditions such as pneumonia or encephalitis. Seek immediate medical attention if symptoms significantly worsen or if severe symptoms develop (e.g., difficulty breathing, severe headache, confusion).\n\nPlease note that this treatment plan is based on the limited information provided and should not replace a comprehensive evaluation by a healthcare professional. The patient should seek immediate medical attention if symptoms worsen or if any red flags are present.', NULL, 992, 992, '2025-10-09 20:39:27', '2025-10-09 20:40:05', 98),
(41, 54, '44', 'fever', 'Patient Information:\nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon\nAge: 33\nGender: Male\nMedical History: Not clearly provided\n\nCurrent Symptoms:\nFever\n\n1. Potential Differential Diagnoses:\n   - Viral Infection: Common cold, influenza, COVID-19\n   - Bacterial Infection: Streptococcal pharyngitis, urinary tract infection, pneumonia\n   - Other Infectious Diseases: Malaria, dengue fever, typhoid\n   - Autoimmune Diseases: Rheumatoid arthritis, lupus\n   - Malignancies: Leukemia, lymphoma\n\n2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests:\n   - Complete Blood Count (CBC)\n   - Blood Culture\n   - Urinalysis\n   - Chest X-ray (if respiratory symptoms are present)\n   - PCR test for COVID-19 (considering the current pandemic situation)\n   - Malaria Parasite Identification (if the patient has a travel history to endemic areas)\n\n3. Urgency Level: \n   - Medium: Fever is a common symptom but can indicate a variety of conditions, some of which may be serious. It\'s important to monitor the patient\'s condition and conduct appropriate tests to identify the cause.\n\n4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes:\n   - The patient\'s medical history is not clearly provided, which limits the ability to identify potential risk factors or pre-existing conditions that may be relevant.\n   - The symptom of fever is nonspecific and can be associated with a wide range of conditions. Further information about accompanying symptoms, duration, and pattern of fever would be helpful in narrowing down the differential diagnoses.\n\n5. Red Flags to Watch For:\n   - High fever (>103°F or 39.4°C)\n   - Fever lasting more than a week\n   - Severe headache\n   - Shortness of breath\n   - Chest pain\n   - Abdominal pain\n   - Confusion or altered consciousness\n   - Rash or other skin changes\n   - Recent travel to disease-endemic areas\n\nPlease note that this analysis is based on the limited information provided and may not cover all possible conditions. It is recommended to consult with a healthcare professional for a proper diagnosis and treatment.', 'nknnkjnk', '', 'Without the doctor\'s clinical input and test results, it\'s challenging to provide a primary diagnosis. However, based on the information given, the patient is suffering from a fever, which could be due to a variety of causes. \n\n1. Primary Diagnosis: \n   - Fever of unknown origin (until further tests are conducted)\n\n2. Treatment Goals:\n   - Alleviate the symptom of fever\n   - Identify and treat the underlying cause of the fever\n   - Prevent potential complications\n\n3. Medication Recommendations:\n   - Paracetamol 500mg every 4-6 hours as needed for fever (not exceeding 4g in 24 hours)\n   - Further medication will depend on the results of the diagnostic tests and the identified cause of the fever\n\n4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions:\n   - Rest and hydration\n   - Cool compresses can be applied to the forehead or back of the neck to help reduce fever\n\n5. Follow-Up Schedule:\n   - Immediate follow-up once test results are available\n   - Further follow-ups will depend on the identified cause of the fever\n\n6. Monitoring Parameters:\n   - Monitor temperature regularly\n   - Monitor for any new or worsening symptoms\n   - Monitor response to treatment\n\n7. Patient Education Points:\n   - Importance of rest and hydration\n   - Proper use of fever-reducing medication\n   - Seek immediate medical attention if symptoms worsen or new symptoms develop\n\n8. Potential Complications to Watch For:\n   - High fever (>103°F or 39.4°C)\n   - Fever lasting more than a week\n   - Severe headache\n   - Shortness of breath\n   - Chest pain\n   - Abdominal pain\n   - Confusion or altered consciousness\n   - Rash or other skin changes\n\nPlease note that this treatment plan is based on the limited information provided and may need to be adjusted based on the results of the diagnostic tests and the identified cause of the fever. It is recommended to consult with a healthcare professional for a proper diagnosis and treatment.', NULL, 992, 992, '2025-10-09 21:39:28', '2025-10-09 21:40:21', 98),
(42, 54, '44', 'fever', 'Patient Information:\nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon\nAge: 33\nGender: Male\nMedical History: Not clearly provided\n\nCurrent Symptoms:\nFever\n\n1. Potential Differential Diagnoses:\n   - Viral infection (e.g., influenza, common cold)\n   - Bacterial infection (e.g., strep throat, urinary tract infection)\n   - COVID-19\n   - Malaria\n   - Autoimmune diseases (e.g., lupus, rheumatoid arthritis)\n   - Certain types of cancer (e.g., lymphoma)\n   - Other infectious diseases (e.g., tuberculosis, HIV)\n\n2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests:\n   - Complete blood count (CBC)\n   - Blood culture\n   - Urinalysis\n   - Chest X-ray (if respiratory symptoms are present)\n   - COVID-19 PCR test\n   - Malaria test (if travel history to endemic areas)\n\n3. Urgency Level: \n   - Medium: Fever is a common symptom of many conditions, some of which can be serious. It\'s important to identify the cause and start appropriate treatment.\n\n4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes:\n   - The patient\'s medical history is unclear, which makes it difficult to narrow down potential causes of the fever. More information is needed about the duration and severity of the fever, associated symptoms, recent travel history, and potential exposure to infectious diseases.\n   - The patient\'s age and gender may also influence the differential diagnosis. For example, urinary tract infections are less common in young, healthy males.\n\n5. Red Flags to Watch For:\n   - High fever (>103°F or 39.4°C)\n   - Fever lasting more than a week\n   - Severe headache\n   - Shortness of breath\n   - Chest pain\n   - Confusion or altered mental status\n   - Rash or other skin changes\n   - Severe abdominal pain\n   - Signs of dehydration (e.g., dry mouth, decreased urination)\n\nPlease note that this analysis is based on limited information and should not replace a thorough clinical evaluation. The patient should consult with a healthcare professional for proper diagnosis and treatment.', 'viral infection', '', 'Based on the doctor\'s clinical input and the patient\'s symptoms, the primary diagnosis is a viral infection.\n\n1. Primary Diagnosis:\n   - Viral Infection\n\n2. Treatment Goals:\n   - Alleviate symptoms\n   - Prevent complications\n   - Prevent transmission to others\n\n3. Medication Recommendations:\n   - Acetaminophen 500mg every 4-6 hours as needed for fever. Do not exceed 3000mg in 24 hours.\n   - Over-the-counter cold remedies may help with other symptoms if present (e.g., cough, congestion). Follow package instructions for dosing.\n\n4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions:\n   - Rest and hydration\n   - Isolation to prevent transmission to others\n   - Use of a humidifier may help with respiratory symptoms\n\n5. Follow-Up Schedule:\n   - If symptoms worsen or do not improve within 5-7 days, seek medical attention.\n   - If symptoms resolve, no follow-up is necessary unless new symptoms develop.\n\n6. Monitoring Parameters:\n   - Monitor temperature regularly\n   - Monitor for worsening or new symptoms (e.g., difficulty breathing, severe headache, rash)\n\n7. Patient Education Points:\n   - Viral infections are typically self-limiting and resolve with supportive care.\n   - It\'s important to stay hydrated and rest.\n   - Over-the-counter medications can help manage symptoms but will not shorten the duration of the illness.\n   - Prevent transmission to others by practicing good hygiene (e.g., hand washing) and staying home while sick.\n\n8. Potential Complications to Watch For:\n   - Dehydration due to fever and decreased fluid intake\n   - Secondary bacterial infection\n   - Pneumonia (if respiratory symptoms develop)\n   - Worsening or persistent symptoms may indicate a more serious condition and should be evaluated by a healthcare professional.\n\nPlease note that this treatment plan is based on the assumption of a common viral infection. If the patient\'s symptoms worsen or do not improve within a week, or if new symptoms develop, they should seek medical attention. This plan should not replace a thorough clinical evaluation and consultation with a healthcare professional.', NULL, 992, 992, '2025-10-09 21:48:29', '2025-10-09 21:49:01', 98),
