ever', '', 'Based on the provided information, here is a comprehensive treatment plan:\n\n1. Primary Diagnosis:\n   - Viral Fever\n\n2. Treatment Goals:\n   - Alleviate symptoms, particularly fever\n   - Prevent complications\n   - Ensure patient comfort and recovery\n\n3. Medication Recommendations:\n   - Paracetamol 500mg-1g every 4-6 hours as needed for fever (not to exceed 4g in 24 hours)\n   - Hydration with oral rehydration salts if signs of dehydration are present\n\n4. Non-Pharmacological Interventions:\n   - Adequate rest and sleep\n   - Maintain hydration, preferably with water, clear broths, or rehydration drinks\n   - Balanced diet to support immune function\n\n5. Follow-Up Schedule:\n   - Follow-up after 48-72 hours to assess response to treatment\n   - Immediate follow-up if symptoms worsen or new symptoms develop\n\n6. Monitoring Parameters:\n   - Monitor temperature regularly\n   - Monitor for signs of dehydration\n   - Monitor for the development of new symptoms or worsening of existing symptoms\n\n7. Patient Education Points:\n   - Importance of taking medication as prescribed\n   - Importance of rest and hydration\n   - When to seek immediate medical attention (e.g., high fever, severe headache, shortness of breath, chest pain, confusion, severe weakness)\n\n8. Potential Complications to Watch For:\n   - Dehydration\n   - Secondary bacterial infection\n   - Progression of the viral illness\n\nPlease note that this treatment plan is based on the limited information provided and the primary diagnosis of viral fever. It is important to reassess the patient\'s condition regularly and adjust the treatment plan as necessary. Always consult with a healthcare professional for proper diagnosis and treatment.', 'Medications:\n1. Paracetamol - 500mg-1g - Every 4-6 hours as needed for fever - Until fever subsides\n2. Oral Rehydration Salts - As directed on the package - As needed if signs of dehydration are present - Until symptoms of dehydration subside\n\nInstructions:\n- Take Paracetamol as needed for fever, but do not exceed 4g in a 24-hour period.\n- Use Oral Rehydration Salts as directed on the package if signs of dehydration are present.\n- Ensure adequate rest and sleep.\n- Maintain hydration, preferably with water, clear broths, or rehydration drinks.\n- Follow a balanced diet to support immune function.\n\nPrecautions:\n- Monitor temperature regularly.\n- Watch for signs of dehydration.\n- Be alert for the development of new symptoms or worsening of existing symptoms.\n- Seek immediate medical attention if you experience high fever, severe headache, shortness of breath, chest pain, confusion, or severe weakness.', 992, 992, '2025-10-09 22:36:42', '2025-10-09 22:37:59', 98),
(48, 54, '44', 'fever', 'Patient Information:\nName: Rizvi Mahmud Plabon\nAge: 33\nGender: Male\nMedical History: Not clearly provided\n\nCurrent Symptoms:\nFever\n\n1. Potential Differential Diagnoses:\n   - Viral infection (e.g., influenza, common cold)\n   - Bacterial infection (e.g., strep throat, urinary tract infection)\n   - COVID-19\n   - Malaria\n   - Other infectious diseases (e.g., tuberculosis, HIV)\n   - Autoimmune diseases (e.g., lupus, rheumatoid arthritis)\n   - Malignancies (e.g., lymphoma, leukemia)\n\n2. Recommended Diagnostic Tests:\n   - Complete Blood Count (CBC)\n   - Blood culture\n   - Urinalysis\n   - Chest X-ray\n   - COVID-19 PCR test\n   - Malaria parasite test\n   - If fever persists, further tests may be needed such as autoimmune markers, HIV test, TB test, etc.\n\n3. Urgency Level: \n   - Medium: Fever is a common symptom but can indicate a serious condition if it persists or is accompanied by other severe symptoms.\n\n4. Key Observations and Clinical Notes:\n   - The patient\'s medical history is not clearly provided, which limits the ability to identify potential risk factors or pre-existing conditions that may contribute to the current symptom.\n   - The symptom of fever is very nonspecific and can be associated with a wide range of conditions, from minor viral infections to serious systemic diseases.\n\n5. Red Flags to Watch For:\n   - High fever (>103°F or 39.4°C)\n   - Fever lasting more than a week\n   - Severe headache\n   - Neck stiffness\n   - Shortness of breath\n   - Chest pain\n   - Abdominal pain\n   - Confusion or altered consciousness\n   - Rash or spots on skin\n   - Severe vomiting or diarrhea\n\nPlease note that this analysis is based on the limited information provided and may not cover all possible conditions. It is recommended that the patient consult with a healthcare professional for a proper diagnosis and treatment.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 992, NULL, '2025-10-10 05:41:14', NULL, 98);
